Clear the host bits of an httpd A: or D: network address.
A client matches a rule when its address ANDed with the mask equals the
rule's address, which was stored as written. A rule such as
"A:192.168.1.5/24", with bits set outside the mask, therefore matched
no client at all, silently.

Mask the address when the rule is parsed, as is usual for a network
written this way.

---
 networking/httpd.c |    2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

--- a/networking/httpd.c
+++ b/networking/httpd.c
@@ -955,6 +955,8 @@
 				pip->ip = 0; /* could be set before error is detected - zero it (again) */
 				pip->mask = 0;
 			}
+			/* "10.1.2.3/8" means the network 10.0.0.0/8 */
+			pip->ip &= pip->mask;
 			//bb_error_msg_and_die("ip:0x%08x mask:0x%08x", pip->ip, pip->mask);
 			pip->allow_deny = ch;
 			if (ch == 'D') {
